Analysis Pipeline

The pipeline consists of eight stages.

Stage 1 — Configuration

Loads:

ohira.config.ts

package.json

Environment variables

CLI flags


Output:

{
  "projectRoot": "...",
  "plugins": [],
  "cache": true
}


---

Stage 2 — Project Discovery

The Scanner detects:

Language

Runtime

Framework

Package Manager

Repository

Docker

ORM

CI/CD


Example:

Node.js

↓

Next.js

↓

Prisma

↓

Docker

↓

Git


---

Stage 3 — Plugin Resolution

Determine which plugins are applicable.

Example:

package.json

↓

Next Plugin

Prisma Plugin

Docker Plugin

Git Plugin

Plugins that do not support the current project are skipped.


---

Stage 4 — Collection

Each plugin extracts structured information.

Example:

Prisma:

Models

Enums

Relations

Migrations


Git:

Branch

Commits

Contributors


Docker:

Services

Images

Ports



---

Stage 5 — Normalization

Different technologies describe similar concepts differently.

Example:

Prisma

model User

Drizzle

table users

Both become:

Entity(User)

Normalization ensures the Knowledge Graph uses a common vocabulary.


---

Stage 6 — Graph Construction

Collected entities become nodes.

Relationships become edges.

Example:

User

owns

Booking

references

Property


---

Stage 7 — Insight Generation

The engine derives higher-level information.

Examples:

Unused services

Missing documentation

Circular dependencies

Authentication coverage

Technical debt indicators

Architecture summaries



---

Stage 8 — Output Generation

Outputs include:

JSON

Markdown

HTML

AI Context

REST responses



---

Plugin Execution

Plugins execute independently.

Core

↓

Plugin Manager

↓

Git Plugin

↓

Prisma Plugin

↓

Docker Plugin

↓

Next Plugin

Each plugin returns:

KnowledgeNode[]

The Core combines all nodes into one graph.


---

Plugin Scheduling

The scheduler classifies plugins into three groups:

Discovery Plugins

Examples:

Node

Git


Run first.


---

Dependency Plugins

Examples:

Prisma

Docker

Clerk


Run after discovery.


---

Insight Plugins

Examples:

Health

Documentation

Security


Run last.


---

Parallel Execution

Independent plugins may run concurrently.

Example:

Git Plugin

Prisma Plugin

Docker Plugin

↓

Parallel

↓

Merge Results

This reduces total analysis time.


---

Cache Manager

Ohira caches expensive operations.

Examples:

package.json parsing

Git metadata

Prisma schema parsing

Docker inspection


Cache is invalidated only when relevant files change.


---

Incremental Analysis

Instead of rebuilding everything:

schema.prisma changed

↓

Only Prisma Plugin reruns

↓

Graph updates

↓

Reports regenerate

This keeps repeated analyses fast.


---

Error Recovery

Errors should never stop the entire pipeline.

If a plugin fails:

Plugin Error

↓

Log

↓

Disable Plugin

↓

Continue Analysis

The user receives a warning, but analysis completes whenever possible.


---

Dependency Injection

The Core provides shared services through dependency injection.

Available services:

Logger

Cache

File System

Event Bus

Configuration

Metrics


Plugins request services rather than creating their own implementations.


---

Event Bus

Major events emitted by the Core:

analysis.started

plugin.loaded

plugin.failed

graph.updated

report.generated

analysis.completed

Future plugins can subscribe to these events.


---

Metrics Collection

The Core tracks:

Analysis duration

Plugin execution time

Files analyzed

Nodes generated

Relationships created

Cache hit rate

Memory usage


These metrics help optimize performance over time.


---

Concurrency Model

Rules:

Independent plugins execute in parallel.

Dependent plugins execute sequentially.

Graph updates are synchronized.

Shared resources are protected from race conditions.


This balances performance with correctness.


---

Logging

Every analysis receives a unique ID.

Example:

Analysis ID:
anl_20260806_001

Log levels:

ERROR

WARN

INFO

DEBUG

TRACE


This simplifies troubleshooting and auditing.


